@flexiarg institutions/appropriation-and-provision
@title Appropriation And Provision
@keywords proportionality, fairness, provision, appropriation, Ostrom 2B, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 2B, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/benefit-proportional-to-contribution]

! conclusion:
  "The benefits obtained by users from a common-pool resource (CPR), as determined by appropriation rules, are proportional to the amount of inputs required in the form of labor, material, or money, as determined by provision rules."

  + context:
    Appropriation rules say what users may take; provision rules say what they must put in.

  + IF:
    Users both draw on a resource and contribute to its upkeep.

  + HOWEVER:
    Contributions differ in kind (labour, material, money) and are hard to compare; strict proportionality can be costly to measure.

  + THEN:
    Link what each user may take to what each user puts in, in terms the users accept as comparable.

  + BECAUSE:
    Users who see their benefits as fair relative to their inputs keep contributing; perceived unfairness erodes compliance.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/benefit-proportional-to-contribution.]
